1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,600
Sam wants to reduce his drug use.

2
00:00:02,980 --> 00:00:06,465
He has tried several times, but familiar places and difficult

3
00:00:06,480 --> 00:00:11,205
days bring strong urges. Missed appointments and strained relationships

4
00:00:11,220 --> 00:00:12,760
are adding to the pressure.

5
00:00:13,040 --> 00:00:16,140
He wants help that makes everyday life more manageable.

6
00:00:16,740 --> 00:00:20,605
Drug use disorders involve harmful patterns of use and difficulty

7
00:00:20,620 --> 00:00:25,265
regulating them. Biology, learning and circumstances interact.

8
00:00:25,280 --> 00:00:29,485
Effective treatment depends on the drug involved, the person's

9
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:31,660
health and what keeps the problem going.

10
00:00:32,135 --> 00:00:36,060
This is general information for adults, not medical advice.

11
00:00:36,520 --> 00:00:38,660
Withdrawal can require medical care.

12
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:42,540
Do not abruptly stop prescribed medicines because of a video.

13
00:00:43,060 --> 00:00:46,360
A qualified professional can help plan safer treatment.

14
00:00:47,320 --> 00:00:51,560
Opioids, stimulants, cannabis and sedatives have different effects.

15
00:00:51,900 --> 00:00:54,565
Their risks and treatments are not interchangeable.

16
00:00:54,580 --> 00:00:58,040
Alcohol has its own episode in this series.

17
00:00:58,520 --> 00:01:03,360
Using several substances together makes assessment and treatment more complicated.

18
00:01:03,920 --> 00:01:06,300
Physical dependence is a separate question.

19
00:01:06,740 --> 00:01:10,305
The body can adapt to a prescribed medicine, causing withdrawal

20
00:01:10,320 --> 00:01:13,980
if it is stopped. That can happen during appropriate treatment.

21
00:01:14,520 --> 00:01:19,440
Withdrawal or needing a different dose does not, by itself, establish addiction.

22
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:24,180
Assessment asks what is being used, how often, in what combinations,

23
00:01:24,460 --> 00:01:28,425
and with what consequences. It includes withdrawal, physical

24
00:01:28,440 --> 00:01:32,360
and mental health, other medicines and the person's circumstances.

25
00:01:32,780 --> 00:01:36,140
The aim is an honest picture and a useful plan.

26
00:01:36,960 --> 00:01:40,405
Inherited differences can affect vulnerability through sensitivity

27
00:01:40,420 --> 00:01:43,300
to drugs, behaviour and mental health.

28
00:01:43,700 --> 00:01:46,640
Many genetic differences contribute small effects.

29
00:01:47,100 --> 00:01:51,805
Family history raises risk, but exposure and circumstances influence

30
00:01:51,820 --> 00:01:56,280
what happens. A person's future is not decided by their genes.

31
00:01:57,400 --> 00:02:00,665
Availability, social networks, adversity and opportunities for

32
00:02:00,680 --> 00:02:02,440
a rewarding life also matter.

33
00:02:02,960 --> 00:02:06,525
Some people use drugs for pleasure, some for relief, and many

34
00:02:06,540 --> 00:02:10,845
for several reasons. Trauma is an important risk factor, but

35
00:02:10,860 --> 00:02:12,800
it does not explain every case.

36
00:02:13,660 --> 00:02:17,905
Drugs can strongly reinforce behaviour: their effects make repeating

37
00:02:17,920 --> 00:02:22,165
the action more likely. Places, people and feelings can become

38
00:02:22,180 --> 00:02:26,225
linked with those effects. Later, these cues may trigger wanting

39
00:02:26,240 --> 00:02:29,880
even when the experience brings less enjoyment than expected.

40
00:02:30,380 --> 00:02:34,300
Repeated exposure can also change how brain cells communicate.

41
00:02:34,880 --> 00:02:38,925
Some changes contribute to tolerance, withdrawal or difficulty

42
00:02:38,940 --> 00:02:43,065
regulating responses. These changes differ between drugs and

43
00:02:43,080 --> 00:02:48,680
people. There is no single permanent dopamine level that explains every addiction.

44
00:02:49,220 --> 00:02:52,665
Brain changes can make stopping difficult without making recovery

45
00:02:52,680 --> 00:02:56,985
impossible. New learning, treatment and changed circumstances

46
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:02,005
can support recovery. Persistent memories or urges do not mean

47
00:03:02,020 --> 00:03:05,100
that someone will inevitably return to harmful use.

48
00:03:05,925 --> 00:03:09,965
For opioid use disorder, methadone and buprenorphine are the

49
00:03:09,980 --> 00:03:14,200
main established medicines. They can reduce withdrawal and craving,

50
00:03:14,480 --> 00:03:18,245
support continued treatment and reduce non-prescribed opioid

51
00:03:18,260 --> 00:03:22,645
use. Studies also find substantially lower death rates while

52
00:03:22,660 --> 00:03:24,160
people remain in treatment.

53
00:03:24,687 --> 00:03:28,500
The clinician and patient choose together, considering safety,

54
00:03:28,740 --> 00:03:31,205
previous response and practical access.

55
00:03:31,220 --> 00:03:35,005
Treatment can continue for as long as it remains beneficial.

56
00:03:35,020 --> 00:03:39,585
Counselling and practical support should be offered, but unavailable

57
00:03:39,600 --> 00:03:42,680
counselling should not delay effective medication.

58
00:03:43,240 --> 00:03:47,145
Detoxification alone is not sufficient continuing treatment for

59
00:03:47,160 --> 00:03:51,180
opioid use disorder. Tolerance falls after stopping.

60
00:03:51,600 --> 00:03:55,240
Returning to a previously used amount can then cause an overdose.

61
00:03:55,800 --> 00:03:59,220
A planned transition into ongoing care is important.

62
00:03:59,692 --> 00:04:03,200
Naloxone can temporarily reverse an opioid overdose.

63
00:04:03,760 --> 00:04:07,625
People at risk, and those around them, can ask local services

64
00:04:07,640 --> 00:04:12,445
about access and training. Suspected overdose requires emergency

65
00:04:12,460 --> 00:04:15,140
help even if naloxone has been given.

66
00:04:15,640 --> 00:04:17,860
Protection and treatment belong together.

67
00:04:18,725 --> 00:04:22,705
For cocaine and other stimulant disorders, the strongest behavioural

68
00:04:22,720 --> 00:04:24,945
evidence is for contingency management.

69
00:04:24,960 --> 00:04:30,380
This means prompt, tangible rewards for agreed, verified progress,

70
00:04:30,580 --> 00:04:33,820
such as attending treatment or drug-negative tests.

71
00:04:34,260 --> 00:04:37,800
It usually sits alongside practical psychological support.

72
00:04:38,280 --> 00:04:41,805
Talking treatment can help people recognise triggers, manage

73
00:04:41,820 --> 00:04:44,340
urges and build rewarding alternatives.

74
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:48,425
Some medicines show benefits for selected stimulant problems,

75
00:04:48,440 --> 00:04:52,025
but their effects are generally more modest and uncertain.

76
00:04:52,040 --> 00:04:56,820
There is no universally agreed first-choice medicine for these disorders.

77
00:04:57,380 --> 00:05:01,045
For cannabis use disorder, treatment commonly combines help with

78
00:05:01,060 --> 00:05:05,165
motivation and cognitive behavioural therapy: practical work

79
00:05:05,180 --> 00:05:07,500
on thoughts, habits and coping.

80
00:05:07,940 --> 00:05:11,360
The evidence is less certain than for opioid medicines.

81
00:05:11,880 --> 00:05:16,560
No medication has an established first-line role for cannabis use disorder.

82
00:05:16,951 --> 00:05:20,425
Dependence on certain sedatives, including some medicines for

83
00:05:20,440 --> 00:05:25,160
anxiety and sleep, may need a gradual, individually planned reduction.

84
00:05:25,720 --> 00:05:29,540
Abrupt withdrawal can cause seizures or dangerous confusion.

85
00:05:30,140 --> 00:05:35,860
Withdrawal from GHB can also deteriorate quickly and needs specialist assessment.

86
00:05:36,760 --> 00:05:41,220
Continuing care also addresses housing, relationships, work,

87
00:05:41,440 --> 00:05:43,765
infection prevention and other health conditions.

88
00:05:43,780 --> 00:05:46,840
These are part of making treatment workable.

89
00:05:47,260 --> 00:05:50,745
Residential care may help some people, but the treatment delivered

90
00:05:50,760 --> 00:05:54,420
and follow-up afterwards matter more than the setting alone.

91
00:05:54,900 --> 00:05:59,580
If use continues or returns, review the plan, safety and support.

92
00:06:00,080 --> 00:06:03,080
Automatic discharge can remove care when it is needed.

93
00:06:03,580 --> 00:06:07,565
Progress may include fewer harms, better health, staying engaged

94
00:06:07,580 --> 00:06:11,580
and greater stability, alongside reduced use or abstinence.

95
00:06:12,205 --> 00:06:15,965
For Sam, recovery means more than getting through a few drug-free

96
00:06:15,980 --> 00:06:19,340
days. It means rebuilding a life he can sustain.

97
00:06:19,840 --> 00:06:23,465
The most useful plan combines treatment supported by evidence

98
00:06:23,480 --> 00:06:27,900
with practical opportunities, continuing support and room to adjust.
